This paper investigates the normalized ground state solution for the given biharmonic Choquard type equation \begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
  \Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),
  \quad\mbox{in}\ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
  \displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^4}|u|^2dx=c^2,\quad u\in H^2(\mathbb{R}^4),
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \end{split}
  \end{align*} under specific constraints and growth conditions for $\beta$, $\lambda$, and $f$, and we leverage a homotopy-based minimax principle to establish the existence of a normalized ground state solution for this equation. This investigation is an extension of a previous study by R. A. Good and Z. P. Wang, which dealt with the case of biharmonic equations in bounded domains. Notably, our research demonstrates that the normalized ground state solution can be obtained without the constraint of subcritical growth, and this equation shares a common structure with the Brézis-Nirenberg type equation, which has been widely studied. Additionally, our research implies the validity of a local minimax principle for this type of equation. As an exploratory investigation, we have not attempted to delve into the intricacies of this topic. Further research is required to fully explore the implications and applications of these results. As an aside, we have also demonstrated that the normalized ground state solution is an isolated critical point for the associated functional when $\beta$ satisfies a certain condition, which can be inferred from a previous study by J. Yang and J. Zhang. It is also noteworthy that this research has an additional significance as it addresses a problem that was previously indeterminate according to the conditions specified by M. Miyamoto and W. M. Z. Zhang. As an added remark, we have also established the existence of a minimum value for the energy associated with this equation without adhering to the constraint of subcritical growth. It is also worth mentioning that this research has an added importance as it deals with an equation that cannot be solved according to the conditions set by M. Miyamoto and W. M. Z. Zhang. As an added observation, we have also demonstrated

